sentences.l:
But Patroclus, to the horses and to Automedon having given orders,
after the Trojans and Lycians went, and greatly was blinded,
foolish;

Adrastus first, and Autonous, and Echeclus,
and Perimus, Megas’-son, and Epistor, and Melanippus,
but then Elasus and Mulius and Pylartes;
these he took;

And Hector in the Scaean Gates held the solid-hoofed horses;
for he was in two minds whether he should fight in the turmoil, having driven in again,
or call the people with a shout to crowd into the wall.

So having spoken, he, the god, went back again into the toil of men;
and Cebriones he ordered, wise-minded, glorious Hector,
the horses into the war to lash.

And both his brows the stone crushed together, and not for him did hold
the bone, and his eyes to the ground fell in the dust
right there before his feet;

The two, around Cebriones, like two lions contended,
who on the peaks of a mountain, around a slain deer,
both hungry, both greatly thinking, fight;
